14. What are time complexity and space complexity?

Time Complexity is the measure of the amount of time an algorithm takes to complete its execution as the input size increases. It helps compare the efficiency of different algorithms.

Space Complexity is the amount of memory required by an algorithm while it is running. It includes both the memory needed for input data and any extra memory used during execution.

Both time and space complexity are important for selecting efficient algorithms.

---

15. Explain Big-O, Omega and Theta notations.

Big-O (O) Notation represents the upper bound or worst-case performance of an algorithm. It shows the maximum time an algorithm may take.

Omega (Ω) Notation represents the lower bound or best-case performance. It indicates the minimum time required by an algorithm.

Theta (Θ) Notation represents the tight bound, meaning the algorithm performs within both the upper and lower bounds. It gives the most accurate representation of an algorithm's growth rate.

For example, if an algorithm always takes proportional to n operations, its complexity is Θ(n), while its worst case is O(n) and its best case is Ω(n).

