3. Linear Search and Binary Search

(A) Linear Search Algorithm

START
Input array A, size n and ITEM.

For i = 0 to n-1
    If A[i] == ITEM
        Print "Element Found"
        STOP
    End If
End For

Print "Element Not Found"
STOP

Time Complexity

- Best Case: O(1)
- Worst Case: O(n)

---

(B) Binary Search Algorithm

Binary Search works only on a sorted array.

START
Set LOW = 0
Set HIGH = n-1

While LOW <= HIGH
    MID = (LOW + HIGH)/2

    If A[MID] == ITEM
        Print "Element Found"
        STOP

    Else If ITEM < A[MID]
        HIGH = MID - 1

    Else
        LOW = MID + 1
End While

Print "Element Not Found"
STOP

Time Complexity

- Best Case: O(1)
- Worst Case: O(log n)

Comparison

Feature| Linear Search| Binary Search
Array Required| Sorted or Unsorted| Sorted Only
Worst Case| O(n)| O(log n)
Best Case| O(1)| O(1)
Method| Checks one by one| Divides array into halves

