9. Priority Queue: Array vs Heap Implementation

A Priority Queue is a special type of queue in which each element has a priority. The element with the highest (or lowest) priority is removed before other elements.

(A) Array Implementation

Insertion

Insert the new element at the end of the array.

Time Complexity: O(1)

Deletion

Search for the highest-priority element, remove it, and shift the remaining elements.

Time Complexity: O(n)

---

(B) Heap Implementation

A Binary Heap is a complete binary tree that satisfies the heap property.

Insertion

Insert the new element at the last position and move it upward (heapify up).

Time Complexity: O(log n)

Deletion

Remove the root element, replace it with the last element, and restore the heap (heapify down).

Time Complexity: O(log n)

---

Comparison Table

Operation| Array| Heap
Insertion| O(1)| O(log n)
Deletion| O(n)| O(log n)
Find Highest Priority| O(n)| O(1)

Conclusion

- Array implementation is simple and suitable for small datasets.
- Heap implementation is more efficient for frequent insertion and deletion operations, making it the preferred choice in real-world applications such as CPU scheduling and graph algorithms.

