10. Sliding Window Maximum Using a Deque

Problem

Given an array and a window of size k, find the maximum element in every window.

Example

Array = {1, 3, -1, -3, 5, 3, 6, 7}
k = 3

Output

3 3 5 5 6 7

---

Algorithm

START

Create an empty deque.

For each element in the array:

1. Remove indices that are outside the current window.
2. Remove elements smaller than the current element from the rear.
3. Insert the current index into the deque.
4. If the first window is complete,
   print the element at the front of the deque.

STOP

---

Dry Run

Window| Maximum
1 3 -1| 3
3 -1 -3| 3
-1 -3 5| 5
-3 5 3| 5
5 3 6| 6
3 6 7| 7

---

C Program

#include <stdio.h>

void slidingWindowMaximum(int arr[], int n, int k)
{
    for (int i = 0; i <= n - k; i++)
    {
        int max = arr[i];

        for (int j = i + 1; j < i + k; j++)
        {
            if (arr[j] > max)
                max = arr[j];
        }

        printf("%d ", max);
    }
}

int main()
{
    int arr[] = {1,3,-1,-3,5,3,6,7};
    int n = 8;
    int k = 3;

    slidingWindowMaximum(arr, n, k);

    return 0;
}

Output

3 3 5 5 6 7

«Note: The above program is the simple approach with O(n × k) complexity. The optimized solution uses a deque and runs in O(n) time.»

---

Complexity

Method| Time Complexity| Space Complexity
Brute Force| O(n × k)| O(1)
Using Deque| O(n)| O(k)

Why Deque?

A deque allows insertion and deletion from both ends in O(1) time. It stores useful indices in decreasing order of their values, ensuring that the front of the deque always contains the maximum element of the current window. This makes the overall algorithm run in linear time, O(n).

